Stop Gesture from re-dispatching a claimed touch event to outer targets

`Gesture.dispatchEvent()` collects every registered `Gesture.addTarget`
element that contains the touch point, sorts them nearest-ancestor first,
and dispatches the same synthetic event (tap, contextmenu, etc.) to each of
them in turn, unconditionally. Many workbench UI parts register both an
inner element and one of its ancestors as separate Gesture targets, each
with its own handler for the same event type — for example an editor tab
(`.tab`) and its enclosing `.tabs-container` both listen for
`TouchEventType.Contextmenu` and each open a context menu. A single
long-press therefore fires two competing `showContextMenu()` calls in the
same synchronous loop; the second call's `ContextViewService` view replaces
the first, so the menu that briefly appeared for the correct (inner) target
immediately closes. This reproduces on long-press across the editor,
explorer, and tab bar, matching the "context menu opens then instantly
closes" report.

The fix stops the dispatch loop once a handler has claimed the event via
`preventDefault()`, mirroring standard DOM propagation semantics: once
something has handled the gesture, ancestors should not also react to it.
This generalizes the one-off `defaultPrevented` guard already carried by
`SubmenuMenuActionViewItem.onClick` (see responsive-workbench.diff) into a
single fix at the dispatch source instead of scattering guards across every
callsite.

Index: code-server-mobile/lib/vscode/src/vs/base/browser/touch.ts
===================================================================
--- code-server-mobile.orig/lib/vscode/src/vs/base/browser/touch.ts
+++ code-server-mobile/lib/vscode/src/vs/base/browser/touch.ts
@@ -302,6 +302,9 @@ export class Gesture extends Disposable
 			for (const [_, target] of targets) {
 				target.dispatchEvent(event);
 				this.dispatched = true;
+				if (event.defaultPrevented) {
+					break;
+				}
 			}
 		}
 	}
